Integrating an auction-driven recommendation LLM into event planning.

0. A disclaimer

I don’t have any experience writing research proposals and am unfamiliar with the format, so I utilised University of Birmingham’s guidelines (“How to write a research proposal”) as a loose inspiration, keeping the style rather non-formal. I’m keeping this brief both due to unfamiliarity with the format and the project’s objective being yet to discuss - wouldn’t want to spend much time on something just to rewrite it whole. This will be polished if necessary based on your feedback.

1. Topic

Generally speaking, the idea I’ve got is to use our LLM to find best fit locations (as in countries, regions, cities depending on the datasets used) for events based on their format (musical tours, business fairs, charity events) and requirements. AI-based event manager runs an analysis of user’s preferences and demands against the available dataset, outputting suggestions both what locations best fit the search and how the event’s plan could be tweaked to fit a larger selection of dataset entries. 

The user could choose between consulting 1 LLM agent or picking X LLM agents to “roleplay” different stakeholders (for example, one LLM agent prioritising event’s affordability and suggesting changes to cut the costs, with the 2nd agent focusing on providing participants with the best experience), thus emulating a board of directors.

It’s important to keep in mind that this task mostly defines the datasets used and characterizes the project’s “flavour”, while the necessary technical achievement will mostly be fulfilled by realizing the auction model for LLM agents.

2. Literature review

While most of the research & papers me and Kaiji have discussed previously covered fashion, music and art recommendations, it would be a challenge to find the right evaluation for such data, since a correct answer rarely exists and such recommendations are subjective - which would be a problem since I’ll need to prove the project’s relevance to the inspecting staff.

Looking at AgentRecBench and SynthTRIPs per my curator’s recommendation inspired me to consider working with data such as geographical locations where there exist more objective metrics. My project would make use of that to create a new solution for event management sphere, utilising LLM agents to ensure deeper and more efficient analysis.

3. Objectives

Along with starting some practical work, urgently find more relevant research to form a better understanding of the status quo and similar existing solutions, identify more specifics unsatisfactions and narrow our objective
Keep looking for similar relevant datasets
Establish metrics for evaluating the model’s performance
Build an MVP and keep training it to work with more datasets, perhaps perform a wider task


4. Strategy

Kaji’s auction model will be used as a founding principle for the project. Open-source models from big corporations will be used as the LLM agents (gpt-oss, qwen etc.). I was recommended to perform ablation studies to tune the results.

5. Anticipated results

Successfully building functional interactive collection of LLM agents capable of rationally calculating and outputting recommendations to the task described in Section 1 to the user while focusing to minimize token costs and maximise efficiency according to chosen metric.

6. Schedule and budget

Since it is first and foremost an educational project, financial investments are unlikely to be needed - what will be needed is external computational power, which is being arranged at the moment.

As for the timeline, this proposal is being written and sent on January 26 with the intention of making preparations for commencing the practical part the following day and the final task being defined by the end of the same week.

With the submission deadline being March 27, the practical part should commence by the end of January. The MVP creation should take place in the first half of February, with the later half being dedicated to refining the project. Final touch-ups will be made in the first week of March with the rest of the month being used to write the final report and research.

2 meetings will be commenced weekly to track the project’s progress and make corrections to the roadmap, if necessary.

